# November 21, 2026: Washington state (region wa), claimed with tools/choose.py --thinnest because Washington had the fewest
# places of any state or province (18, nearly all in Jefferson County and Olympia). This batch spreads a first scatter
# across the rest of the state: the Seattle gas plant turned park, Tacoma's Union Station, the Wilkeson coke ovens,
# the Hudson's Bay Company fort at Vancouver, the Columbia River forts (Fort Columbia) and Fort Casey on Whidbey Island,
# Northern State Hospital, the Monte Cristo mining townsite, Roslyn and its 26 cemeteries, Liberty, Fort Simcoe, the
# Maryhill Stonehenge, the Whitman Mission site, the Dayton depot, the Pomeroy and Waterville courthouses, Fort Spokane,
# the Barron flour mill at Oakesdale, and Old Molson. Sources opened in this session: Wikipedia as raw text
# (tools/wiki_raw.py) and its Register lists; the National Register form for the Barron mill (NPGallery, text layer read);
# Washington State Parks park pages; National Park Service pages for Fort Vancouver, Whitman Mission and Fort Spokane;
# Seattle Parks and Recreation; the Mount Baker-Snoqualmie National Forest trail page; Molson Museums; the Dayton
# Chamber of Commerce; the Port of Skagit; the Port of Whitman County; the Mountains to Sound Greenway Trust; the
# Spokesman-Review, the Courier-Herald and Oregon Public Broadcasting.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'wa_wxtp','label':'Washington: a first scatter','added':'2026-11-21 06:32'}
WSP='Washington State Parks'
DP='A Discover Pass is needed to park (one day $10, a year $45, from the park page read November 2026).'
def NR(r): return L('National Register nomination form '+r,'https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/'+r+'_text')
PLACES=[
N('gas-works-park-seattle','Gas Works Park','Seattle','wa','industry','ruins',47.64513,-122.33489,'exact','public','walk','','1906–1956; park 1975',
  'The Seattle Gas Light Company made gas from coal, and later from oil, on this point on the north shore of Lake Union from 1906 to 1956. The city bought the site in 1962, and landscape architect Richard Haag kept the plant\'s towers and buildings in the park that opened in 1975. Wikipedia calls it the remnants of the sole remaining coal gasification plant in the United States. It was put on the National Register in 2013.',
  'Six generator towers with steel shells over brick linings (the two biggest, of 1937 and 1938, are 80 and 75 feet tall; the four smaller ones date from 1947), with the oil absorber and cooler towers beside them. The old exhauster-compressor house is the children\'s play barn, and the boiler house is the picnic shelter, with the tubes of one boiler still in place at its east end. The "Great Mound" kite hill is made of rubble from the plant\'s foundations.',
  'The only coal gas plant left in the country, kept as a city park.',
  'A Seattle city park, open 6 a.m. to 10 p.m. (Seattle Parks page read November 2026). Entering the lake, wading, fishing and launching boats are banned because the lake bed is contaminated; a sediment clean-up runs from 2026 to 2030. The towers are not for climbing: Wikipedia counts at least 14 falls since 2008, three of them fatal. Parking lot on North Northlake Way, on the Burke-Gilman Trail.',
  [W('Gas Works Park'),L('Seattle Parks and Recreation: Gas Works Park','https://seattle.gov/parks/find/parks/gas-works-park')],
  brick='some',hours='6 a.m. to 10 p.m. daily'),
N('union-station-tacoma','Union Station','Tacoma','wa','rail','standing',47.24592,-122.43633,'exact','view','walk','stations','1911',
  'The Northern Pacific made Tacoma the end of its transcontinental line in 1873, and in 1906 the architects Reed and Stem were chosen to design a station worthy of the city. Union Station opened in 1911. The last passenger train left on June 14, 1984; the concourse was torn down that summer and the empty building decayed. It was restored and has housed a federal courthouse since 1992.',
  'A reinforced-concrete building faced in red brick laid in Flemish bond on a limestone base, under a copper-clad dome 90 feet high that is part of the Tacoma skyline. Wikipedia says the rotunda under the dome is visited by up to 300 people a day in summer; when and how the public may go in was not checked, so interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working federal courthouse (U.S. District Court, Western District of Washington), at Pacific Avenue in downtown Tacoma. Look from the street; entry to the rotunda and its rules were not checked. Street parking.',
  [W('Union Station (Tacoma, Washington)')],
  brick='yes'),
N('wilkeson-coke-ovens','Wilkeson coke ovens','Wilkeson','wa','industry','ruins',47.10198,-122.03947,'exact','public','walk','','1885 or 1890–1935',
  'Wilkeson Coal and Coke ran 160 beehive ovens here, the main supply of coke on the coast, cooking coal for two or three days and sending the coke by rail to Tacoma. Wikipedia says the ovens were built in 1885; the Courier-Herald (2010) says they were built in stages from 1890 with firebrick from Scotland and masons from Pennsylvania. The mine and ovens shut down in 1935, and the sandstone wall that faced the row was taken away in the late 1940s. The 30 ovens left went on the National Register in 1974.',
  'About 30 brick beehive ovens, each about 8 feet high and 12 feet across, covered in cement with a hole on top, in a row in the town\'s Coke Oven Park at the north end of Wilkeson. In 2008 and 2010 the Courier-Herald reported them overgrown and graffitied and the town seeking grants to stabilise some; their present state was not checked.',
  '',
  'Coke Oven Park, a 10-acre park owned by the Town of Wilkeson just outside the town limits (Courier-Herald, 2010), near the elementary school; the town holds its handcar races there. Hours not checked. Street parking in Wilkeson.',
  [W('Wilkeson Coke Ovens'),W('Wilkeson, Washington'),L('Courier-Herald: Coke Oven Park on the endangered list (2010)','https://www.courierherald.com/?p=16592'),L('Courier-Herald: New hope for historic coke ovens (2008)','https://www.courierherald.com/?p=17965')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('fort-vancouver-wa','Fort Vancouver','Vancouver','wa','fort','standing',45.62250,-122.66250,'site','open','walk','forts','1824–1860; rebuilt',
  'The Hudson\'s Bay Company built Fort Vancouver in the winter of 1824 to 1825 as the headquarters of its fur trade west of the Rockies, named for the explorer George Vancouver. The U.S. Army built Vancouver Barracks beside it in 1849, the company left in June 1860, and a fire destroyed the old fort in 1866. The National Park Service has rebuilt the stockade and buildings on their original sites.',
  'The rebuilt fort: every structure in the stockade is a modern replica placed where the original stood (Wikipedia). The visitor centre has rotating exhibits from the site\'s excavations. The barracks and the Pearson Air Museum are in the same park.',
  'The fur trade capital of the Pacific Northwest, rebuilt on its own footprint.',
  'Fort Vancouver National Historic Site, National Park Service. The grounds are open every day from dawn to dusk; the reconstructed fort and visitor centre Tuesday to Saturday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., and all facilities shut on Thanksgiving, Christmas Eve and Day and New Year\'s Eve and Day (NPS hours page read November 2026). Fees not checked.',
  [W('Fort Vancouver'),W('Fort Vancouver National Historic Site'),L('NPS: Fort Vancouver hours','https://www.nps.gov/fova/planyourvisit/hours.htm')],
  th=[45.62420,-122.66501],thn='East 5th Street lot',brick='no',hours='Reconstructed fort and visitor centre Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.; grounds dawn to dusk daily'),
N('fort-casey-wa','Fort Casey and Admiralty Head Light','Coupeville','wa','fort','standing',48.16208,-122.67646,'site','public','walk','forts','1897–1953',
  'Begun in 1897 as the Whidbey Island corner of the "triangle of fire" with Fort Worden and Fort Flagler across Admiralty Inlet. Its big guns on disappearing carriages were active from 1901, but most were taken away in the First World War to serve as railway guns and the rest were scrapped in the Second. The 1903 Admiralty Head lighthouse stands in the park, which is part of Ebey\'s Landing National Historical Reserve.',
  'The concrete batteries, with two 10-inch disappearing guns at Battery Worth and two 3-inch guns at Battery Trevor, all brought from Fort Wint in the Philippines in the 1960s. The lighthouse tower can be climbed, five people at a time, noon to 4 p.m.: in 2026 weekends in March, October and December, Friday to Monday in April, May and September, Thursday to Monday in June, every day in July and August. State Parks said in November 2026 it could not offer guided fort or lighthouse tours.',
  'Disappearing guns that still rise from their pits.',
  'Fort Casey Historical State Park, Washington State Parks, next to the Coupeville ferry terminal. Open 8 a.m. to dusk all year. '+DP+' Navy jets from Naval Air Station Whidbey Island may fly over.',
  [W('Fort Casey'),L(WSP+': Fort Casey','https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/fort-casey-historical-state-park')],
  th=[48.16048,-122.67743],thn='Fort Casey park lot',brick='?',hours='Park 8 a.m. to dusk daily; lighthouse noon to 4 p.m. on set days, March to December'),
N('fort-columbia-wa','Fort Columbia','Chinook','wa','fort','standing',46.25314,-123.91996,'site','open','walk','forts','1896–1950',
  'A Coast Artillery post built from 1896 to 1904 on Chinook Point, with Fort Canby and Fort Stevens one of three forts guarding the mouth of the Columbia River. It was declared surplus after the Second World War and passed to the state in 1950. In the 1960s and 1970s one battery was fitted out as a civil defence emergency centre for the governor. State Parks calls it one of the most intact coastal defence sites in the country.',
  'Twelve wood-frame fort buildings, the batteries with panels of historic photographs and blueprints, and two 6-inch guns brought from Newfoundland in 1993. The interpretive centre and the furnished Commanding Officer\'s House are open Thursday to Sunday, June 1 to August 31, 11 a.m. to 3 p.m. Two fort houses are let as holiday houses.',
  '',
  'Fort Columbia Historical State Park, 475 State Route 101, Chinook, Washington State Parks; U.S. 101 runs in a tunnel under it. Open 6:30 a.m. to 9:30 p.m. in summer and 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. in winter. '+DP+' No fires; dogs on leash. Parking below the fort on the park lanes.',
  [W('Fort Columbia State Park'),L(WSP+': Fort Columbia','https://parks.wa.gov/506/Fort-Columbia')],
  th=[46.25365,-123.92056],thn='Fort Columbia park lot',brick='?',hours='Grounds summer 6:30 a.m. to 9:30 p.m., winter 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.; interpretive centre and house Thursday to Sunday 11 to 3, June to August'),
N('northern-state-hospital','Northern State Hospital','Sedro-Woolley','wa','inst','standing',48.53056,-122.20667,'site','public','walk','','1912–1973',
  'A state mental hospital opened in 1912 to relieve crowding at Western State Hospital. Seattle architects Saunders and Lawton designed it in the Spanish Colonial Revival style and the Olmsted Brothers laid out the grounds; it had its own farm with barns and a milk house. By 1917 it held about 750 patients. Governor Dan Evans closed it for budget reasons, and the last patients left on August 16, 1973. The Port of Skagit has owned the campus since 2018 as the SWIFT Center.',
  'The stuccoed Spanish Colonial Revival ward buildings, assembly hall and service buildings on the Olmsted grounds, seen from the designated outdoor areas. The hospital cemetery is mentioned in Wikipedia; where it is and whether it can be visited was not checked. Interiors are closed.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Port of Skagit (SWIFT Center), off SR 20 about 4 miles northeast of central Sedro-Woolley. The Port opened designated outdoor areas to the public in 2019, dawn to dusk only, and does not allow the public inside buildings (Port page read November 2026). Tenants work here: keep to the open areas.',
  [W('Northern State Hospital'),L('Port of Skagit: SWIFT Center','https://portofskagit.com/swift-center/')],
  th=[48.53152,-122.20650],thn='Hub Drive lot',brick='no',hours='Designated outdoor areas dawn to dusk'),
N('monte-cristo-wa','Monte Cristo townsite','Monte Cristo','wa','town','ruins',47.98556,-121.39389,'area','public','hike','','1889–1983',
  'Joseph Pearsall found glittering ore at the head of the South Fork Sauk River in 1889, and Monte Cristo boomed as a gold and silver camp until 1907, with the Everett and Monte Cristo Railroad built up the valley to serve it. Many miners left for the Klondike by 1900. The town lived on as a resort until the county road washed out in December 1980 and its last business, a lodge, burned in 1983. Arsenic and other mine waste were cleaned up from 2012, and the site reopened on June 3, 2016.',
  'Very few original buildings still stand (Wikipedia). The walk in follows the old railroad grade, past the site of the Weden House station at 1.7 miles, to the Forest Service campground at 4 miles, once part of the Rattler claim. Do not drink the surface water: it carries arsenic and heavy metals that filtering and boiling will not remove.',
  'A four-mile walk up an old railroad grade to a silver camp the river cut off.',
  'Mount Baker-Snoqualmie National Forest (Darrington Ranger District). Park at Barlow Pass on the Mountain Loop Highway (a valid recreation pass is needed) and walk the gated Monte Cristo Road, four miles each way. There is no bridge over the South Fork Sauk River at about one mile: you ford or cross on fallen logs. Some land along the road is private (patented mining claims and summer cabins). The highway east of Bedal closes for winter (it closed November 16, 2026). Forest Service page read November 2026.',
  [W('Monte Cristo, Washington'),L('Forest Service: Old Monte Cristo Townsite Trail','https://www.fs.usda.gov/r06/mbs/recreation/trails/old-monte-cristo-townsite-trail')],
  th=[48.02673,-121.44416],thn='Barlow Pass trailhead',brick='no'),
N('roslyn-historic-district','Roslyn historic district','Roslyn','wa','town','standing',47.22298,-120.99333,'site','public','walk','','1886',
  'Roslyn was platted in 1886 by the Northern Pacific Coal Company to dig coal for the railroad then crossing Stampede Pass. In 1888 the company brought in Black miners to break a strike, and their families founded lodges and an African Methodist Episcopal church; in 1892 an explosion in Mine No. 1 killed 45 men, the worst mining disaster in Washington. The whole 1914 town plat, the cemeteries and the old rail corridor were listed as a historic district in 1978, and the town stood in for Cicely, Alaska, in Northern Exposure.',
  'The company town as it stands: the Northwestern Improvement Company Store, the company store and hub of the town, on its own Register listing at First Street and Pennsylvania Avenue, and the miners\' houses round it. Interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'A small city: walk the public streets. Street parking downtown.',
  [W('Roslyn, Washington')],
  brick='?'),
N('roslyn-cemeteries','Roslyn Historic Cemeteries','Roslyn','wa','burial','standing',47.21996,-120.99867,'site','public','walk','cemeteries','1887',
  'Twenty-six separate cemeteries side by side on about 15 acres southwest of town, holding an estimated 5,000 graves of at least 24 nationalities. From 1887 the coal company gave or sold plots to the lodges and clubs that miners joined by nationality or faith, and each buried its own members. The 45 men killed in the 1892 mine explosion lie in their own lodges\' sections. No Asian people were buried here, though Chinese placer miners came before the coal mines.',
  'Gravestones in 26 plots, each with its own lodge or group, many still privately held by those groups (Wikipedia). The markers of the 1892 dead can still be found and read (Mountains to Sound Greenway).',
  '',
  'Part of the Roslyn historic district, listed in 1978; most sections are owned by the lodges that founded them. Hours and rules not checked: visit in daylight and treat the graves with care.',
  [W('Roslyn Historical Cemeteries'),L('Mountains to Sound Greenway Trust: Roslyn Historic Cemeteries','https://mtsgreenway.org/location/roslyn-historic-cemeteries/')],
  brick='no'),
N('liberty-wa','Liberty','Liberty','wa','town','standing',47.25361,-120.66528,'site','view','drive','','1873; 1895',
  'Gold was panned where Williams Creek meets Swauk Creek from 1867, and a two-dollar nugget found in 1873 started the Swauk mining district. The camp was named Liberty in 1892; from 1895 the settlement moved two miles up Williams Creek to Meaghersville, which took the name, and the last building at Old Liberty burned in 1962. The Klondike drew miners away by 1901, and a gold dredge brought in 1926 proved too big for the creek. Liberty is known for crystalline gold and Ellensburg Blue agates.',
  'The Liberty Historic District (1974): a two-storey hotel and boarding house and a grocery store of the 1890s, an 1894 butcher shop, a stage office, and a miners\' log house moved here from the Old Liberty mine in 1944, along the one road through town, about a third of a mile long. OPB counted a population of 12 in 2023. Interiors not checked.',
  'A gold camp with a dozen people still living in it.',
  'People live here: look from the road. Who owns each building was not checked.',
  [W('Liberty, Washington'),L('OPB: Washington\'s living ghost town (2023)','https://www.opb.org/article/2023/10/29/washington-only-living-ghost-town-history-lingering-residents/')],
  brick='no'),
N('fort-simcoe-wa','Fort Simcoe','White Swan','wa','fort','standing',46.34256,-120.83920,'site','open','drive','forts','1856–1859',
  'The Army built Fort Simcoe in 1856 in an oak grove by springs on the Yakama Nation\'s travel route between the Yakima Valley and the Columbia fisheries, to watch the tribes. Its military life ended in 1859, when it was handed to the Bureau of Indian Affairs as the Indian agency, and it became a school where Native children were forced to give up their customs and language. The agency moved to Toppenish in 1922; the state park opened in 1953. It is one of the few pre-Civil War forts left in the West.',
  'Five original buildings: the commander\'s house, three captains\' houses and one blockhouse, with the tribal jail and rebuilt barracks; like Fort Dalles it had blockhouses at the corners and no stockade. Wikipedia says the interpretive centre, the commander\'s house and two officers\' houses, furnished, open April to September, Wednesday to Sunday, and that the blockhouse is not open; the park page says to call to book a tour of the commander\'s quarters.',
  '',
  'Fort Simcoe Historical State Park, 5150 Fort Simcoe Road, 8 miles west of White Swan, on the Yakama Reservation. Washington State Parks: day use only, 8 a.m. to dusk in summer, closed October 1 to March 31. '+DP+' No alcohol; dogs on leash; bears visit. Tours (509) 874-2372.',
  [W('Fort Simcoe'),L(WSP+': Fort Simcoe','https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/fort-simcoe-historical-state-park')],
  th=[46.34354,-120.83736],thn='Fort Simcoe park lot',conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='April to September 8 a.m. to dusk; closed October 1 to March 31. Commander\'s quarters by tour'),
N('maryhill-stonehenge','Maryhill Stonehenge','Maryhill','wa','landmark','standing',45.69438,-120.80603,'exact','public','drive','','1918–1929',
  'Sam Hill, who also built the Maryhill Museum, put up a full-size concrete copy of Stonehenge as a memorial to the men of Klickitat County killed in the First World War: dedicated on July 4, 1918, the first such memorial in the country, and finished in 1929. A Quaker, he meant it as a warning that people are still being sacrificed to war. The town of Maryhill around it burned, leaving the monument. It was listed on the National Register in 2021.',
  'The concrete ring of stones and lintels on a bluff above the Columbia, with its altar stone set to the summer solstice sunrise, and memorials nearby to the county\'s dead of later wars. It belongs to the Maryhill Museum of Art.',
  'A Quaker\'s Stonehenge, built as a warning against war.',
  'Part of the Maryhill Museum of Art; free, open every day from 7 a.m. to dusk (Spokesman-Review, 2024; the museum\'s own page not read). Parking lot beside it on Stonehenge Drive.',
  [W('Maryhill Stonehenge'),L('Spokesman-Review: visit the first WWI memorial (2024)','https://www.spokesman.com/stories/2024/may/25/this-memorial-day-visit-the-nations-first-wwi-memo/')],
  th=[45.69504,-120.80579],thn='Stonehenge Drive lot',brick='no',hours='7 a.m. to dusk daily'),
N('whitman-mission','Whitman Mission (Waiilatpu)','Walla Walla','wa','worship','marker',46.04340,-118.46517,'site','public','walk','','1836–1847',
  'Marcus and Narcissa Whitman, Presbyterian missionaries, set up their mission at Waiilatpu in Cayuse country in 1836, and it became a stop on the Oregon Trail from 1843. Measles killed half the local Cayuse in November 1847, and some blamed the Whitmans; on November 29 the Whitmans and eleven others were killed and 47 people taken hostage. The killings led Congress to make Oregon a territory and started the Cayuse War. The site became a national monument in 1936 and a national historic site in 1963.',
  'The mission buildings are gone; the site has been dug for artefacts and covered again. Trails cross the grounds, there is a museum, and a memorial obelisk put up fifty years after the killings stands on the hill, a favourite place to watch the sunset.',
  '',
  'Whitman Mission National Historic Site, National Park Service, 328 Whitman Mission Road, west of Walla Walla. The grounds and trails are open every day from sunrise to sunset (NPS hours page read November 2026); visitor centre hours not checked. Parking lot by the visitor centre.',
  [W('Whitman Mission National Historic Site'),L('NPS: Whitman Mission hours','https://www.nps.gov/whmi/planyourvisit/hours.htm')],
  th=[46.04202,-118.46425],thn='Visitor centre lot',brick='no',hours='Grounds sunrise to sunset daily; visitor centre not checked'),
N('dayton-depot-wa','Dayton Historic Depot','Dayton','wa','rail','standing',46.32092,-117.98007,'site','open','walk','stations','1881',
  'Built in 1881 in the Stick/Eastlake style, it is the oldest surviving passenger station in Washington (Dayton Chamber of Commerce). It is on the National Register as the Oregon Railway and Navigation Company Depot. Restored, it is now a museum.',
  'The restored wooden depot, with changing exhibits in the upstairs gallery. Original fittings inside not checked.',
  'The oldest railway station left in the state.',
  'Run by the Dayton Historical Depot Society, 222 E Commercial Avenue, Dayton; phone 509-382-2026. Hours from the Dayton Chamber of Commerce page read November 2026; admission not checked. Street parking.',
  [L('Dayton Chamber of Commerce: Historic Depot','https://www.historicdayton.com/historic-depot')],
  brick='no',hours='May to October Wednesday to Saturday 10 to 5; November to April Wednesday to Saturday 11 to 4; closed January'),
N('garfield-county-courthouse-wa','Garfield County Courthouse','Pomeroy','wa','civic','standing',46.47500,-117.60111,'exact','view','walk','','1901',
  'A fire in 1900 destroyed much of Pomeroy\'s commercial centre and the wooden courthouse of 1887. The county voted a $20,000 bond, Charles Burggraf of Albany, Oregon designed the new one, and August Ilse of Spokane built it in 1901. A clock was put in the tower in 1904. It was listed on the National Register in 1974 and restored over two years, finished in 2011.',
  'Brick from a local kiln on stone from Valentine Ridge by the Snake River, with a clock tower capped by a statue of Justice who, unusually, wears no blindfold. Interior not checked.',
  'A Justice with her eyes open.',
  'The county courthouse, at 8th and Main streets, Pomeroy. Look from the street; office hours not checked.',
  [W('Garfield County Courthouse (Washington)')],
  brick='?'),
N('douglas-county-courthouse-wa','Douglas County Courthouse','Waterville','wa','civic','standing',47.64601,-120.06799,'exact','view','walk','','1905',
  'Built in 1905 to replace a wooden courthouse, to a design by Newton Gauntt, in Waterville, the seat of Douglas County. It was listed on the National Register in 1975.',
  'A two-storey brick Victorian building about 60 feet square. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'The county courthouse, 203 South Rainier Street, Waterville. Look from the street; office hours not checked.',
  [W('Douglas County Courthouse (Washington)')],
  brick='?'),
N('fort-spokane','Fort Spokane','Miles','wa','fort','standing',47.90460,-118.30915,'area','public','walk','forts','1880–1929',
  'Founded in 1880 as Camp Spokane above the meeting of the Spokane and Columbia rivers, the last Army frontier post in the Northwest, set between the Colville and Spokane reservations and the new city of Spokane. It grew to about 50 buildings. The troops left for the Spanish-American War in 1898 and the post became an Indian boarding school (1900 to 1907 says the Park Service; 1899 to 1914 says Wikipedia), then a tuberculosis hospital until 1929. The National Park Service took it over in 1960.',
  'Four original buildings: the 1884 stable, the 1888 powder magazine, the 1889 reservoir and the brick guardhouse (Wikipedia dates it 1892, the Park Service 1880), now the visitor centre and museum with exhibits on the soldiers and the boarding school. The grounds have walking trails.',
  '',
  'Lake Roosevelt National Recreation Area, National Park Service, 44150 District Office Lane N. The grounds are open all year; the guardhouse museum opens in summer, free. It closed in March 2026 for repairs to its porch, roof, windows and brick and was expected to reopen in July; whether it did was not checked.',
  [W('Fort Spokane'),L('NPS: Fort Spokane','https://www.nps.gov/laro/learn/historyculture/fort-spokane.htm'),L('NPS: museum closed for repairs (March 2026)','https://nps.gov/laro/learn/news/fort-spokane-museum-and-visitor-center-to-close-for-repairs.htm')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('barron-flour-mill','J. C. Barron Flour Mill','Oakesdale','wa','industry','standing',47.13018,-117.24201,'site','view','walk','','1890',
  'A timber-framed flour mill of 1890 with its steam plant of the same year, a crib grain elevator of 1898 and a warehouse, beside the old Northern Pacific tracks. The Spokesman-Review says it was first built in Illinois, taken down piece by piece and put back up in Oakesdale. It is the last mill of its kind in Whitman County. In 2024 the Innovia Foundation bought it with donors\' help to save it, to pass to the Port of Whitman County, which won a $1 million state grant in January 2026 for a new roof and to stabilise the outside.',
  'Four storeys of wood towering over the centre of Oakesdale. The 1978 Register form found the milling machinery in place: sacking machines, a small stone buhr mill dated 1871, four double stands of roller mills and a Carter disc cleaner. Whether it is all still inside was not checked. No future use has been decided.',
  'An 1890 flour mill with its machinery still in it, waiting to be saved.',
  'Owned by the Port of Whitman County or the Innovia Foundation (the transfer was planned in 2024; not checked), at First and Jackson streets. Not open: Oakesdale\'s mayor told the Spokesman-Review visitors often ask whether it is. Look from the street. The town holds Old Mill Days in July.',
  [NR('78002788'),L('Spokesman-Review: Port plans to restore Oakesdale mill (2024)','https://spokesman.com/stories/2024/oct/03/port-of-whitman-county-plans-to-restore-oakesdale-'),L('Port of Whitman County: CERB grant (2026)','https://www.portwhitman.com/press-releases/port-of-whitman-county-awarded-cerb-rural-ports-grant')],
  brick='no'),
N('old-molson-wa','Old Molson ghost town','Molson','wa','town','standing',48.97632,-119.19970,'site','open','drive','','1900',
  'Promoter George Meacham and investor John W. Molson of the Molson banking family founded the mining town in 1900; it reached about 300 people that year, then the company withdrew and by June 1901 twelve were left. The Great Northern arrived in 1905 and the town revived, but a quarrel with the townsite owner moved most people half a mile north to New Molson in 1909. The railway was torn up in the 1930s and the post office closed in 1967. In 1960 Harry Sherling began the Old Ghost Town Museum on the original townsite.',
  'An open-air museum of pioneer buildings, farm machinery and mining equipment on the first townsite, with the Molson State Bank among them (pictured on Wikipedia), and two blocks away the Molson Schoolhouse Museum in the old school, with tools, household things and photographs. Most buildings have a step up and no ramp; the equipment sheds are open.',
  'A ghost town that moved half a mile and left its first self behind as a museum.',
  'Molson Museums, 539 Molson Road, about 15 miles northeast of Oroville by the Chesaw Road. Free, donations welcome. The ghost town is open in daylight all year, but the road is not ploughed after snow; the schoolhouse museum is open 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. daily from Memorial Day to Labor Day (museum page read November 2026).',
  [W('Molson, Washington'),L('Molson Museums: hours and access','https://molsonmuseums.org/?p=19')],
  brick='?',hours='Ghost town daylight all year; schoolhouse museum 10 to 5 daily, Memorial Day to Labor Day'),
]
